          %h2#Preface Preface
          %p
            The sections to follow give a summary of the account in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Barandes2025"} Barandes 2025
          %p
            For a finite (physical) system with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \in \mathbb{N}
            states, a one-step random evolution is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+matrix"} stochastic matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P_{i j})_{i, j =1}^N
            whose entry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{i j} \in [0,1]
            gives the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            that the system, if in state
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            Assuming there is a (temporal)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of such one-step random evolutions then these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+matrices"} stochastic matrices
            depend on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of (time) parameters
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≥
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1 \geq t_0 \in \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Say that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            defined thereby is
            %em divisible
            iff these matrices of transition probabilities over some (time) interval are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+multiplication"} matrix product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \cdot (-)
            over those associated with any decomposition of the interval:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext decomposable
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi t
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{decomposable}
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\Leftrightarrow
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\forall_{t_1 \leq t \leq t_0}
                  \\;\;
                  P(t_1, t_0) = P(t_1, t) \cdot P(t, t_0)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now, if these transition probabilities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            are given by the rules of coherent time-evolution of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            according to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born+rule"} Born rule
                says that the probabilities are
                = succeed "-squares" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi Θ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      P_{i j} = \vert\Theta_{i j}\vert^2
              %p
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
                known as
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
                and traditionally denoted as on the right here:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi Θ
                                %mrow
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi j
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi i
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi Θ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi j
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi P
                                %mrow
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi j
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi i
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi Θ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi j
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi j
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %msup
                                %mi Θ
                                %mo †
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi i
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{aligned}
                      \\Theta_{i j}
                      \&amp; = \langle i \vert \Theta \vert j \rangle
                      \\\
                      P_{i j}
                      \&amp; =
                      \\langle i \vert \Theta \vert j \rangle
                      \\,
                      \\langle j \vert \Theta^\dagger \vert i \rangle
                      \\end{aligned}
              %p
                (where traditionally one writes “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                ” for where Barandes has “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
                ”),
            %li
              %p
                (what is essentially) the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
                says that it is these
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
                that compose under
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+multiplication"} matrix multiplication
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi t
                          %mo ≤
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi Θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\forall_{t_0 \leq t \leq t_1}
                      \\;\;
                      \\Theta(t_1, t_0) = \Theta(t_1, t) \cdot \Theta(t, t_0)
                      \\,.
          %p
            But with the probability amplitudes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            composing according to matrix multiplication, then in general the corresponding (doubly) stochastic matrices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of transition probabilities will clearly
            %em not
            compose by the rules of matrix multiplication.
          %p
            In conclusion: The stochastic processes given by quantum processes are generically
            = succeed "." do
              %em indivisible
          %p
            That seems to be the key point highlighted by
            %a{:href => "#Barandes2025"} Barandes 2025
            (without maybe explicitly saying so).
          %blockquote
            %p
