                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(\mathbb{N})
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The indivisible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            proposes that quantum systems can be understood as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
            that lack the “Markov property,” meaning they cannot be broken down into independent, sequential steps. Instead, they evolve over finite chunks of time through a more general, non-Markovian, stochastic law. This interpretation, developed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Barandes"} Jacob Barandes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+interference"} quantum interference
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} decoherence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
            are an artifact of the indivisible dynamics of the underlying indivisible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
            being approximated by Markovian dynamics in the Hilbert space formalism of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %h2#definitions_and_the_stochasticquantum_correspondence Definitions and the stochastic-quantum correspondence
          %p
