                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{i,j}(t)
          %p
            Let us now proceed, from the indivisible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            specified by the laws
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(t)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            formulation of that same process. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{i,j}(t) \in [0,1]
            we can introduce an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ×
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times N
            %strong potential matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(t)
            consisting of complex-valued matrix elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Θ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_{i,j}(t)
            related to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{i,j}(t)
            by the modulus squared relation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Θ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma_{i,j}(t) = |\Theta_{i,j}(t)|^2.
          %p
            The matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(t)
            is not unique. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi diag
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i = diag(0,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0)
            denote the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ×
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times N
            diagonal matrix with value one at row
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and column
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(t)
            as the trace of the composite of four matrices,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi j
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma_{i,j}(t) = tr( \Theta^{\dagger}(t) P_j \Theta(t) P_i )
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta^{\dagger}(t)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugate+transpose+matrix"} conjugate transpose matrix
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(t)
            %strong dictionary formula
            as it provides the translation between indivisible
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            as represented on the right-hand side. This dictionary formula is the basic ingredient of the stochastic-quantum correspondence.
          %p
            Given an initial probability measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(0)
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_0
            with components
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j(0)
            we can define the
            %strong density matrix
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(0) = diag(p_1(0),p_2(0),\ldots,p_N(0))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho(t) = \Theta(t) \rho(0) \Theta^{\dagger}(t)
          %p which is not generally diagonal, but is self-adjoint, positive semi-definite, and has trace equal to one.
          %p
            Provided the density matrix is of rank one we can write the density matrix as the outer product of an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times 1
            column vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi(t)
            and its complex-conjugate-transpose (adjoint)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho(t) = \Psi^{\dagger}(t) \Psi(t).
          %p
            The
            %strong state vector
            or
            %strong wave function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi(t)
            then evolves over time according to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Psi(t) = \Theta(t) \Psi(0),
          %p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi(t)
            satisfies the property
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ||\Psi(t)||=1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            viewpoint of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
          %p
            Note that the measurement process for an indivisible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+process"} stochastic process
            can be modeled within the framework of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G, \eta, \mu)
            as follows:
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi t
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_t \xrightarrow{f} \mathbb{R}
            is a measurable function then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\Omega_t) \xrightarrow{G(f)} G(\mathbb{R})
            just pushes the probability measure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_t
            forward to a probability measure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            in terms of the expected value of the measurement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            then the model of the process, measurement, and computation of the expected value can be displayed within the larger category of algebras of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            as
          %svg{:height => "30.091pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 445.163 30.091", :width => "445.163pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
                %symbol{:id => "pwTdBzspZkahbniVFNt3xpuCcgk=-glyph0-0", :overflow => "visible"}
                  %path{:d => "", :style => "stroke:none;"}
                %symbol{:id => "pwTdBzspZkahbniVFNt3xpuCcgk=-glyph0-1", :overflow => "visible"}
                  %path{:d => "M 4.015625 -7.28125 C 4.015625 -7.609375 4 -7.609375 3.625 -7.609375 C 2.765625 -6.875 1.46875 -6.875 1.21875 -6.875 L 1 -6.875 L 1 -6.375 L 1.21875 -6.375 C 1.625 -6.375 2.234375 -6.4375 2.703125 -6.59375 L 2.703125 -0.5 L 1.09375 -0.5 L 1.09375 0 C 1.578125 -0.015625 2.796875 -0.015625 3.34375 -0.015625 C 3.890625 -0.015625 5.125 -0.015625 5.609375 0 L 5.609375 -0.5 L 4.015625 -0.5 Z M 4.015625 -7.28125 ", :style => "stroke:none;"}
                %symbol{:id => "pwTdBzspZkahbniVFNt3xpuCcgk=-glyph1-0", :overflow => "visible"}
                  %path{:d => "", :style => "stroke:none;"}
                %symbol{:id => "pwTdBzspZkahbniVFNt3xpuCcgk=-glyph1-1", :overflow => "visible"}
